window.INTERVIEW_EXECUTION["swift-70"] = {"timeBudget": {"navigation": 4, "solution": 9, "coding": 26, "verification": 6}, "navigation": {"say": "The question worth asking first is what happens at a shared instant, because it is the whole boundary decision and it changes the answer by a bay. The second is whether the caller wants a number or a plan, because that decides which method is the primitive.", "questions": ["Is a bay freed at the instant its vehicle departs, so an arrival at that same instant can use it?", "Are all bays interchangeable, or do vehicles have constraints on which one they can use?", "Does the depot need to know when the peak occurs and which vehicles cause it, or only how many bays to build?", "Should the assignment be reproducible run to run, or is any valid assignment acceptable?"], "assumptions": ["Sessions are half-open in minutes, so a bay vacated at an instant is available at that instant.", "Bays are interchangeable, which is what makes this a counting question rather than a matching one.", "The planner wants the windows and the vehicles, so the richer method is the primitive and the count is read off it.", "The assignment takes the lowest free bay, so the yard signage is the same every time the plan is regenerated."], "edgeCases": ["an empty schedule, where the depot needs no bays and there is nothing to assign", "a handover at a shared instant, which is the fixture that separates the two event orderings", "the whole fleet returning at once, where the bay count is the fleet size", "sessions that never meet, where one bay serves the entire day"], "restate": "Given each vehicle's charging window, decompose the schedule into ordered events, report how many bays the depot must build, report every stretch at that peak with the vehicles responsible, and assign every vehicle a concrete bay using no more bays than that."}, "solution": {"pitch": "Decompose each session into an arrival and a departure, order them with departures ahead of arrivals at a shared instant, and walk. The counter's maximum is the bay count; the stretches where it sits at that maximum are the peak windows. Both are projections of one walk, which is why they cannot disagree. The fourth question is the one the counter cannot answer, because knowing how many vehicles are parked tells you nothing about which bay each is in, so it gets a heap of occupied bays keyed by release instant and a heap of free indices beside it.", "dataStructures": ["One event array, sorted once. The whole first three parts read it and nothing else.", "A set of the vehicles currently parked, which is what lets a window name its fleet rather than only count it.", "For the assignment, a heap of occupied bays keyed by release instant, so the earliest release is always at hand.", "A second heap of free bay indices, which is what makes the assignment reproducible rather than merely valid."], "types": ["An event is an enum with associated values rather than a struct with a flag, so an arrival and a departure cannot be confused at a call site.", "It exposes its instant and vehicle id as computed properties, because the sort needs both without unwrapping.", "A peak window is a struct carrying the stretch, the occupancy and the vehicles, since a planner needs all three and recovering the third afterwards is another pass.", "The occupied bay is a private Comparable struct, ordered by release instant, which is what lets the provided heap do the work without a custom heap."], "composition": "Parts two and three both read one private walk, and that seam is deliberate rather than incidental: the count is the maximum occupancy the walk reaches and the windows are the stretches that reach it, so there is one sweep in the file and no way for them to disagree. Part four cannot call either of them, since neither hands back a bay, so the suite asserts the composition as an invariant instead: the number of distinct bays used must equal the reported bay count, and no two vehicles sharing a bay may overlap. That second check is the one that catches a plausible assignment, because using the right number of bays and double-booking one is exactly what deriving it from the counter produces.", "tradeoff": "Carrying the parked set through the walk costs a set insertion and removal per event, where a bare counter would be two increments. I want it because the windows are what a planner acts on, and reconstructing them afterwards means a second walk over the same events. If the count alone were ever the whole requirement, the walk projects to it in one line.", "trap": "Two, and the first is a single line in a comparator. At a shared instant departures must come first, and an ordering that leaves that to the sort's stability reports a bay the depot never needs. The second is the complexity: comparing every pair of sessions gives the same answer as the sweep and is quadratic, so at a hundred thousand sessions it is ten billion comparisons. No test here times it, which is exactly why it is worth saying out loud."}, "coding": {"order": ["Write the event decomposition and its comparator first, stating the shared-instant rule aloud as you write it.", "Check it immediately on two back-to-back sessions, which is the schedule the wrong ordering gets wrong.", "Write the walk as one private helper that produces stretches with their fleets, not as a bare counter.", "Read the bay count off it, then the peak windows off it, and notice that neither needs new logic.", "Write the assignment last, with the two heaps, and say why the counter could not have produced it.", "Finish by checking both assignment properties, not just the bay count."], "commentary": ["Each session becomes two events, which is what turns an interval question into a walk.", "At a shared instant the departure comes first, because a bay vacated at that instant is available at that instant.", "That is not a stylistic choice: order them the other way and a schedule where one vehicle hands over to the next appears to need two bays.", "I am making the walk produce stretches with their fleets rather than a bare count, because the next question needs the fleets and I would otherwise walk twice.", "The bay count is the largest occupancy the walk reaches; the peak windows are the stretches that reach it. One sweep, two questions.", "I keep consecutive peak stretches separate, because an event between them changed who is parked and merging them would name a fleet that never existed.", "For the assignment the counter is no help at all. It knows how many vehicles are parked and never which bay each is in.", "So occupied bays go into a heap keyed by when they free, and free bays into a heap keyed by index, which makes the assignment reproducible as well as correct."], "checkIn": "Before the assignment I would say: I am going to hand out the lowest-numbered free bay each time. Any free bay would be equally correct, but a reproducible assignment is worth more to a yard that has to paint the numbers on the floor, and it makes the plan diffable when the schedule changes."}, "verification": {"walkthroughs": ["Walk four sessions: one vehicle from 0 to 30 and three shorter ones at 5 to 10, 15 to 20 and 25 to 40.", "The occupancy runs one, two, one, two, one, two, one, so the depot needs two bays and hits that peak three separate times.", "Reorder the events so arrivals come first at shared instants and watch a two-session handover claim it needs two bays.", "Assign the four sessions: the long vehicle takes bay zero and every short one reuses bay one, because each is released before the next arrives.", "Try deriving that assignment from the counter instead and watch two overlapping vehicles land in the same bay while the bay count still looks right."], "tests": "Run the sizer's suite and read the four parts in order. The first suite's handover fixture is what catches the event ordering, and the second suite cross-checks the sweep against counting occupancy one minute at a time. The fourth suite checks the two assignment properties separately, which is what makes a plausible-but-double-booked assignment red rather than green.", "complexity": "The sort dominates at O(n log n) and the walk is linear in the events. The assignment is O(n log n) as well, from the two heaps. The alternative worth naming and declining is the pairwise overlap count, which is O(n squared) for the identical answer: it passes every functional test in this suite and does not finish at the supported session count, which is why the requirement is stated in the problem rather than enforced by a timing test.", "moreTime": "Bays with different charging rates, which turns the assignment from interchangeable into a matching problem. Then a cost objective, where opening a bay has a capital cost and the question becomes how many bays are worth building rather than how many are needed. And a live schedule, where sessions arrive and the assignment has to be stable enough that a vehicle already told to use bay four is not moved."}, "code": "public struct ChargeSession: Equatable, Sendable {\n    public let vehicleID: String\n    public let arrival: Int\n    public let departure: Int\n\n    public init(vehicleID: String, arrival: Int, departure: Int) {\n        self.vehicleID = vehicleID\n        self.arrival = arrival\n        self.departure = departure\n    }\n}\n\npublic enum ChargeEvent: Equatable, Sendable {\n    case arrives(vehicleID: String, at: Int)\n    case departs(vehicleID: String, at: Int)\n\n    public var instant: Int {\n        switch self {\n        case .arrives(_, let at), .departs(_, let at): at\n        }\n    }\n\n    public var vehicleID: String {\n        switch self {\n        case .arrives(let vehicleID, _), .departs(let vehicleID, _): vehicleID\n        }\n    }\n}\n\npublic struct PeakWindow: Equatable, Sendable {\n    public let start: Int\n    public let end: Int\n    public let occupancy: Int\n    public let vehicleIDs: [String]\n\n    public init(start: Int, end: Int, occupancy: Int, vehicleIDs: [String]) {\n        self.start = start\n        self.end = end\n        self.occupancy = occupancy\n        self.vehicleIDs = vehicleIDs\n    }\n}\n\npublic enum DepotError: Error, Equatable, Sendable {\n    case departureNotAfterArrival(vehicleID: String)\n    case duplicateVehicleID(String)\n    case tooManySessions(Int)\n    case notImplemented\n}\n\n/// A binary min-heap, provided because the standard library has none and\n/// building one is not what this problem is about. Use it, or do not; the\n/// public interface is what the tests read.\npublic struct MinHeap<Element: Comparable>: Sendable where Element: Sendable {\n    private var storage: [Element] = []\n\n    public init() {}\n\n    public var count: Int { storage.count }\n    public var isEmpty: Bool { storage.isEmpty }\n\n    /// The smallest element, without removing it.\n    public var minimum: Element? { storage.first }\n\n    public mutating func insert(_ element: Element) {\n        storage.append(element)\n        var child = storage.count - 1\n        while child > 0 {\n            let parent = (child - 1) / 2\n            guard storage[child] < storage[parent] else { break }\n            storage.swapAt(child, parent)\n            child = parent\n        }\n    }\n\n    public mutating func popMin() -> Element? {\n        guard let smallest = storage.first else { return nil }\n        storage.swapAt(0, storage.count - 1)\n        storage.removeLast()\n\n        var parent = 0\n        while true {\n            let left = parent * 2 + 1\n            let right = left + 1\n            var swap = parent\n            if left < storage.count, storage[left] < storage[swap] { swap = left }\n            if right < storage.count, storage[right] < storage[swap] { swap = right }\n            guard swap != parent else { break }\n            storage.swapAt(parent, swap)\n            parent = swap\n        }\n        return smallest\n    }\n}\n\npublic struct ChargerCapacitySizer: Sendable {\n    /// The day's schedule is bounded, which is also what makes the quadratic\n    /// version of the second part unusable rather than merely slow.\n    public static let maximumSessionCount = 100_000\n\n    public init() {}\n\n    // MARK: Part 1 - Decompose into events\n\n    /// The schedule as the arrivals and departures a sweep walks.\n    ///\n    /// The ordering is the whole boundary decision and it is made once, here.\n    /// At a shared instant departures come first, because a bay vacated at 30\n    /// is available to a vehicle arriving at 30. Leaving that to the sort's\n    /// stability is not a contract, and it is off by one bay on exactly the\n    /// schedules a depot cares about.\n    public func events(from sessions: [ChargeSession]) throws(DepotError) -> [ChargeEvent] {\n        try validate(sessions)\n\n        var timeline: [ChargeEvent] = []\n        timeline.reserveCapacity(sessions.count * 2)\n        for session in sessions {\n            timeline.append(.arrives(vehicleID: session.vehicleID, at: session.arrival))\n            timeline.append(.departs(vehicleID: session.vehicleID, at: session.departure))\n        }\n\n        return timeline.sorted { left, right in\n            if left.instant != right.instant { return left.instant < right.instant }\n            if left.isDeparture != right.isDeparture { return left.isDeparture }\n            return left.vehicleID < right.vehicleID\n        }\n    }\n\n    // MARK: Part 2 - Peak occupancy\n\n    /// How many bays the depot must build so that no returning vehicle waits.\n    ///\n    /// This reads the maximum off the one sweep in the file rather than\n    /// running a sweep of its own. The alternative that suggests itself, one\n    /// comparison per pair of sessions, is correct and quadratic; at the\n    /// supported session count it is ten billion comparisons and no test here\n    /// would tell you.\n    public func requiredBays(_ sessions: [ChargeSession]) throws(DepotError) -> Int {\n        try occupancySegments(sessions).map(\\.occupancy).max() ?? 0\n    }\n\n    // MARK: Part 3 - When, and who\n\n    /// Every stretch of the day at which the depot is at its peak, with the\n    /// vehicles parked during it.\n    ///\n    /// Consecutive stretches stay separate even when both sit at the peak: an\n    /// event between them changed who is parked, and merging them would report\n    /// a set of vehicles that were never simultaneously present.\n    public func peakWindows(_ sessions: [ChargeSession]) throws(DepotError) -> [PeakWindow] {\n        let segments = try occupancySegments(sessions)\n        guard let peak = segments.map(\\.occupancy).max() else { return [] }\n        return segments.filter { $0.occupancy == peak }\n    }\n\n    // MARK: Part 4 - Assign a bay to every session\n\n    /// The bay each vehicle should be sent to, using no more bays than the\n    /// depot must build.\n    ///\n    /// The running counter of the sweep cannot answer this: it knows how many\n    /// vehicles are parked, never which bay each is in. Releasing a bay means\n    /// knowing when its occupant leaves, so the occupied bays live in a heap\n    /// keyed by departure and the free ones in a heap keyed by index, which is\n    /// what keeps the assignment reproducible.\n    public func bayAssignment(_ sessions: [ChargeSession]) throws(DepotError) -> [String: Int] {\n        try validate(sessions)\n\n        let byArrival = sessions.sorted { left, right in\n            left.arrival == right.arrival\n                ? left.vehicleID < right.vehicleID\n                : left.arrival < right.arrival\n        }\n\n        var assignment: [String: Int] = [:]\n        var occupied = MinHeap<OccupiedBay>()\n        var free = MinHeap<Int>()\n        var opened = 0\n\n        for session in byArrival {\n            // Half-open again: a bay whose occupant departs exactly now is\n            // free now, so the release test is `<=` rather than `<`.\n            while let head = occupied.minimum, head.releasesAt <= session.arrival {\n                _ = occupied.popMin()\n                free.insert(head.bay)\n            }\n\n            let bay: Int\n            if let reused = free.popMin() {\n                bay = reused\n            } else {\n                bay = opened\n                opened += 1\n            }\n\n            assignment[session.vehicleID] = bay\n            occupied.insert(OccupiedBay(releasesAt: session.departure, bay: bay))\n        }\n\n        return assignment\n    }\n\n    // MARK: The one sweep\n\n    /// Every stretch between two consecutive events, with who is parked during\n    /// it. Parts 2 and 3 are both projections of this, which is what keeps a\n    /// single sweep in the file.\n    private func occupancySegments(_ sessions: [ChargeSession]) throws(DepotError) -> [PeakWindow] {\n        var segments: [PeakWindow] = []\n        var parked: Set<String> = []\n        var cursor: Int?\n\n        for event in try events(from: sessions) {\n            if let start = cursor, event.instant > start, !parked.isEmpty {\n                segments.append(\n                    PeakWindow(\n                        start: start,\n                        end: event.instant,\n                        occupancy: parked.count,\n                        vehicleIDs: parked.sorted()\n                    )\n                )\n            }\n\n            switch event {\n            case .arrives(let vehicleID, _): parked.insert(vehicleID)\n            case .departs(let vehicleID, _): parked.remove(vehicleID)\n            }\n            cursor = event.instant\n        }\n\n        return segments\n    }\n\n    /// One occupied bay, ordered by the instant it frees up so the heap always\n    /// offers the bay that becomes available soonest.\n    private struct OccupiedBay: Comparable, Sendable {\n        let releasesAt: Int\n        let bay: Int\n\n        static func < (lhs: OccupiedBay, rhs: OccupiedBay) -> Bool {\n            lhs.releasesAt == rhs.releasesAt ? lhs.bay < rhs.bay : lhs.releasesAt < rhs.releasesAt\n        }\n    }\n\n    // MARK: Shared validation\n\n    /// The precondition every part rests on. Parts 2 and 3 reach it through\n    /// the event decomposition; Part 4 does not walk events at all, so it\n    /// calls this directly.\n    private func validate(_ sessions: [ChargeSession]) throws(DepotError) {\n        guard sessions.count <= Self.maximumSessionCount else {\n            throw .tooManySessions(sessions.count)\n        }\n        var seen = Set<String>()\n        seen.reserveCapacity(sessions.count)\n        for session in sessions {\n            guard session.departure > session.arrival else {\n                throw .departureNotAfterArrival(vehicleID: session.vehicleID)\n            }\n            guard seen.insert(session.vehicleID).inserted else {\n                throw .duplicateVehicleID(session.vehicleID)\n            }\n        }\n    }\n}\n\nprivate extension ChargeEvent {\n    /// Departures sort before arrivals at a shared instant, so the sweep needs\n    /// to tell them apart without unwrapping their payloads.\n    var isDeparture: Bool {\n        if case .departs = self { return true }\n        return false\n    }\n}\n"};
